  "worked_solution": "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+10=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by subtracting $10$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+10 & =0 \\\\ -15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}+\\cancel{10}-\\cancel{10} & =0-10 \\\\ -15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $-15,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =-10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =\\frac{10}{15} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1} & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $\\frac{2}{3}$ as ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{-1}$ to get\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x-1}={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(\\frac{3}{2})$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =-1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=0.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
